                <pb facs="0591" n="507" sameAs="frus:frus1886/pg507"/>

                <head>No. 255.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Woodbury to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Boston</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1886-07-07"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 7, 1886</date>.
                        [Received July 10.]</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I forward twelve affidavits of respectable
                    fishermen of Gloucester laying the foundation to show that the Canadian
                    authorities have this year, without notice, radically reversed their
                    administration of laws toward American bait buyers and others. That thereby some
                    of our fishermen have been entrapped and seized is well known to you. I retain a
                    duplicate set, for use in case a suit is brought for a penalty of $400 against
                    an assumed master of the D. J. Adams, which the customs authorities allege has
                    been incurred.</p>
                <p>I have sent these in the expectation that, in the varying phases these Canadian
                    assaults on our commerce assume, these facts may be useful to the Department, if
                    not in the matter of the D. J. Adams.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>CHAS. LEVI WOODBURY.</signed></closer>















